course: history
lesson: 8
title: Ancient India: Ashoka, zero and our numbers
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Ashoka, the grandson of Chandragupta Maurya, conquered the land of Kalinga in a terrible war in which very many people died. Seeing the suffering, he changed his heart.
explain: He had messages carved on rocks and tall pillars across India, asking people to be kind, respect all religions and treat animals well, and he sent teachers to spread the Buddha's teaching as far as Sri Lanka.
explain: Later, under the Gupta kings, came a golden age of science and art. Indian mathematicians treated zero as a real number, and the digits 0 to 9 on your calculator were invented in India.
explain: Think of writing one hundred and five: without a zero, how would you tell 105 from 15? That little circle is one of the cleverest ideas ever, and it reached Europe through Arab scholars, which is why people wrongly call the digits Arabic.
fact: Ashoka, Chandragupta Maurya's grandson, conquered the land of Kalinga in a terrible war in which very many people died.
fact: After the war Ashoka had messages carved on rocks and tall stone pillars across India, asking people to be kind, respect all religions and treat animals well.
fact: Ashoka sent teachers to other lands, including Sri Lanka, to spread the Buddha's teaching.
fact: About 1,600 years ago the Gupta kings ruled northern India in a time of science, art and poetry often called a golden age.
fact: Indian mathematicians were the first to treat zero as a real number you can add and subtract with, writing it as a dot or a circle.
fact: The digits 0 to 9 that we use were invented in India and passed on by Arab scholars to Europe; they are often called Arabic numerals, but they began in India.
fact: The mathematician and astronomer Aryabhata, about 1,500 years ago, explained that the Earth spins and worked out the value of pi very accurately.
fact: An early form of chess called chaturanga was played in India about 1,500 years ago, with pieces like elephants, chariots and foot soldiers.
fact: Chess travelled to Persia, the Arab world and Europe and changed into the game we play today; the chaturanga elephant, standing next to the horse, became today's bishop.
fact: India traded pepper, cinnamon, cotton cloth and precious stones with Rome, Arabia and China; pepper did not grow in Italy, so in Rome it was rare and costly.
fact: India has always been home to many peoples, languages and religions: Hinduism, Buddhism and Jainism began there, Islam and Christianity came later from other lands, and the Sikh faith began in India about 500 years ago.
fact: Ashoka's lion pillar is today a national emblem of India.
exercise: Which land did Ashoka conquer in a terrible war? | Kalinga | Persia | Egypt | Sri Lanka
exercise: How did Ashoka change after the war? | He turned to kindness and peace | He started even more wars | He gave up being king and became a farmer | He left India for Rome
exercise: Where did Ashoka have his messages carved? | On rocks and tall stone pillars | On clay tablets kept in a royal library | On paper books for schools | On the walls of pyramids
exercise: What did Ashoka's messages ask people to do? | Be kind, respect all religions and treat animals well | Pay more taxes and fight harder against the kingdom's enemies | Worship only the king | Build pyramids for him
exercise: Where did Ashoka send teachers of Buddhism? | To other lands such as Sri Lanka | Only to his own palace and nowhere else | To Britain and Ireland | To the Americas
exercise: When was the Gupta golden age? | About 1,600 years ago | About 160 years ago | About 16,000 years ago | About 6,000 years ago
exercise: What did Indian mathematicians do first with zero? | Treat it as a real number to add and subtract | Ban it from all counting | Use it only as a picture of the Sun rising in the morning | Write it as a big X
exercise: Where were our digits 0 to 9 invented? | India | Arabia | Rome | Greece
exercise: How did the Indian digits reach Europe? | Through Arab scholars | Through Viking traders | Through Chinese emperors | Through the Roman army
exercise: What did Aryabhata explain about the Earth? | That it spins | That it is flat | That it stands still | That it is a cube
exercise: Which number did Aryabhata work out very accurately? | Pi | A million | Seven | A billion
exercise: What was chaturanga? | An early form of chess | A kind of spicy curry | A Gupta king | A great temple in Kalinga
exercise: Which pieces were in chaturanga? | Elephants, chariots and foot soldiers | Dragons, wizards and kings with swords | Cars, planes and ships | Lions, tigers and bears
exercise: What did India trade with Rome, Arabia and China? | Pepper, cinnamon and cotton cloth | Potatoes, chocolate and ripe tomatoes | Oil, plastic and coal | Ice, snow and fur
exercise: Which religions began in India? | Hinduism, Buddhism and Jainism | Christianity and Islam | The Norse and Greek religions | None, all came from elsewhere
exercise: What is Ashoka's lion pillar today? | A national emblem of India | A football trophy | A Roman statue standing in Italy | A museum in London
test: Mate says they're Arabic numerals so the Arabs invented 'em. Fair? | No, they began in India and Arabs passed them on | Yes, they began in Arabia | No, the Romans invented them | Yes, and India copied them from the Arabs much later on
test: Try writing one hundred and five with no zero sign. What problem do you hit? | You cannot tell it apart from fifteen | It becomes a million | Nothing, zero is never needed | It turns into a letter
test: Why is Ashoka's change of heart so famous? | A great conqueror chose kindness after seeing suffering | He was the only king in all of history who never fought any war | He won every war with no one hurt | He invented the number zero
test: In modern chess the bishop sits next to the knight. Which chaturanga piece once stood there? | The elephant | The dragon | The camel driver | The wizard
test: If India had never invented zero as a number, what would be harder today? | Writing big numbers and doing sums | Playing football | Growing pepper | Building pillars
test: Why did Roman ladies pay a lot for Indian pepper? | It came from very far and was rare in Rome | Pepper grew everywhere in Italy, so it was a fashion | It was used as money for soldiers | Pepper was banned in India
test: A cartoon says ancient Indians thought the Earth stood still. What did Aryabhata say? | That the Earth spins | That the Earth is flat and still | That the Sun spins round it daily | That the Earth is hollow
test: Ashoka asked people to respect all religions. Why would that matter in India? | India was home to many faiths and peoples | Everyone in India had exactly the same faith already | Religions were banned in India | Only one temple existed
test: How did the Buddha's teaching get to Sri Lanka? | Ashoka sent teachers there | It was found on the Moon | Romans carried it there | Vikings sailed it there
test: blah blah so the gupta age was like the dark ages for india right? | No, it was a golden age of science, art and poetry | Yes, nothing happened at all for hundreds of years | Yes, all books were burned | No, it was the Ice Age
test: Chess went from India to Persia to Europe. What happened to it on the way? | It changed into the game we play today | It stayed exactly the same, piece for piece | It turned into football | It was forgotten until 1990
exam: Identify the ruler who, subsequent to the conquest of Kalinga, caused edicts enjoining kindness and religious tolerance to be inscribed upon pillars. | Ashoka | Chandragupta Maurya | Aryabhata | Cyrus the Great
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of the digits 0 to 9 employed at present. | They originated in India and were transmitted to Europe by Arab scholars | They originated in Arabia without antecedents | They were devised by the Romans | They were devised in Europe in the twentieth century
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the contribution of Aryabhata? | He maintained that the Earth rotates and computed pi with precision | He invented the game of chess | He conquered the land of Kalinga | He founded the Maurya Empire
exam: Ascertain the name of the early Indian game from which modern chess is descended. | Chaturanga | Senet | Go | Mancala
